# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to .env.local and adjust if needed:   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Development runs against Postgres on your own machine. Nobody shares a
# database, and nobody needs credentials to anyone else's cloud account.
# `npm run db:setup` creates the role, the database, the schema and the demo
# data for you.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DATABASE_URL="postgresql://mahek:mahek@127.0.0.1:5432/mahekone"

# Production is Postgres in a container on the DigitalOcean droplet, not
# addressable from the internet — see DEPLOY.md. There is no preview
# environment. Reaching production from a laptop means opening the SSH tunnel
# and pointing DATABASE_URL at it, never pulling credentials into a file here:
#
#   ssh -N -L 5433:127.0.0.1:5432 deploy@<droplet-ip> &
#
# It was Neon behind Vercel until August 2026, and `vercel env pull` is how
# that account's credentials kept reappearing in .env.local long after nothing
# read them. Do not run it.

# Password reset links. Without these the mail is written to the server log
# instead of sent, which is what local development wants — set them and the
# same link goes out through Resend.
# RESEND_API_KEY="re_..."
# MAIL_FROM="MahekOne <no-reply@mahek.in>"

# Dictation — the microphone on every box where somebody writes a sentence.
#
# NOT REQUIRED HERE. Admin Console → Voice sets the same keys into the
# database, which is the way in on a deploy nobody has shell access to, and the
# console wins where both are set. These stay for local development and for
# deploys that already had them.
#
# Sarvam hears the speech: built for Indian languages and for sentences that
# switch language halfway. Its sync endpoint refuses audio over 30 seconds.
# SARVAM_API_KEY="sk_..."
#
# OpenAI takes what Sarvam cannot — anything longer than 30 seconds, and
# anything it fails on — and is the only thing that can do Tighten and Rewrite.
# OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-proj-..."
#
# Which provider is asked first, and which models, are configuration:
# Admin Console → CRM → Voice.

# Only needed where the request host is not the address people click, e.g. a
# tunnel. Otherwise the link is built from the incoming request.
# APP_URL="https://one.mahek.in"

# The leads sheet, read-only. A Google service account that has been given
# Viewer on that one sheet and nothing else — the token is minted with the
# readonly scope, so this cannot write back even if the sharing said it could.
# The private key is the `private_key` field of the service account JSON, with
# its newlines left as literal \n. Keep the quotes.
# Without all three the sync says it is not configured rather than reporting a
# successful sync of nothing.
# GOOGLE_SA_EMAIL="...@....iam.gserviceaccount.com"
# GOOGLE_SA_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
#
# Both workbook ids are HARDCODED — in lib/services/sheet-sync-service.ts for
# orders and lib/services/employee-sync-service.ts for employees. An id names a
# document rather than granting access to one, and the credential above is what
# actually opens it. Set these only to point a deploy at a copy. They are two
# different workbooks: only one carries an Employee Details tab, only the other
# carries current orders, and neither is a fallback for the other.
# ORDERS_SHEET_ID="1AbC..."
# HR_SHEET_ID="1AbC..."
# FIELD_ACTIVITY_SHEET_ID="1AbC..."
#
# The bearer token /api/hrms/sync and /api/sheets/sync require. Nothing calls
# them on a schedule — Vercel Cron is a paid feature — so this is for whatever
# external scheduler or person does. Without it both endpoints refuse rather
# than running open to the world.
# CRON_SECRET="a-long-random-string"
